				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">A bawdy sitar tune drifted throughout the room, as did the light haze of smoke—a byproduct of its patrons.  Business was about usual for such an unassuming time of day and a place that rarely garnered a lot of heavy traffic to begin with.  Still, it was a living.  The establishment had its list of regular clientele, and their thirst for drink and entertainment kept the bills readily paid and the staff fed.  Little more could be expected for such an under-the-table establishment as the ill-reputed basement of the Maiden’s Fancy.  It was one of those places everyone in Neriak knew about, but so rarely admitted to personally gracing.  To do so would imply a number of social truths about the person, and those truths rarely sat well in patrician circles of Teir’Dal society.
<br />

<br />
As proprietor of the sunken hostelry, such concerns never bothered Bramton D’Terrax in the least.  Fame and fortune accorded little to men like he who preferred a lifestyle favoring the idyll, if only in an esoteric way.  His vices were few, but exquisite.  As such, he was king of his particular castle, and the perks he gained were plentiful enough to satisfy.  
<br />

<br />
Bramton leaned against the wooden doorframe confidently as he took stock of the few patrons who sat, drank, and talked amidst the common area.  In one corner sat a group of males playing cards, their quiet diversion interrupted only by the occasional post-hand banter that normally took place.  In the shadowy depths of another corner sat a couple of clandestine businessmen who spoke together in low voices—a sight not atypical for a place where probing questions were the only real taboo.  All other seats lay empty, save for a lonely well-to-do patron—an ambassador, by rumor—who sat near the bar that doubled as a stage for the Maiden’s Fancy’s main attraction.   He was the only customer in the room who paid the slightest bit of attention to the females whose trim, scantily clad, blue-skinned figures gyrated seductively in time with the sitar.  
<br />

<br />
Seeing this, Bramton shook his head.  Sometimes he wondered why he bothered having the girls dance at all.  When it came right down to it, they only served as window dressing most of the time, and a distraction the rest.  He had to remind himself it was a distraction that drew in fresh customers, as well as enticement for the more discreet services rendered at a premium—a lucrative and time-honored enterprise that remained the place’s true cash flow.
<br />

<br />
Footsteps could be heard coming down the stairs—another customer come to wile his time and money away in secrecy, no doubt.  Marvelous.  It was early yet, and it never hurt to make a good start on the day’s take.  This time Bramton figured on greeting this new arrival personally.  After all, he had nothing better to do at the moment.
<br />

<br />
Much to his surprise, the figure that rounded the corner wasn’t male at all.  It was a female, which in itself wasn’t entirely unusual, except this one arrived obviously alone and dressed shoulders to toe in some sort of obsidian armor and helmet.  The ensemble was ornate as it was protective, its chain rings exotically arranged in elaborate patterns and whorls as it clung to the contours of her frame.  What was more interesting was how she came fully armed, as indicated by the two leather-bound scabbards dangling from her hips.  There was certainly no ban on weapons in the Maiden’s Fancy, but most patrons preferred wearing their protection less overtly in this place.
<br />

<br />
Bramton watched curiously as the woman paused in the entryway.  Her back partially turned toward him, she performed a short inspection of the room from where she stood.  If she was a newcomer, one couldn’t tell by her comportment.  Apparently she was looking for someone or something less obvious than the clear and present assets the room had to offer.  Such displays didn’t faze or elicit any response from her at all, in fact.  Instead she overlooked them in search of something else—something specific that eluded her.
<br />

<br />
It wasn’t immediately clear if she found what she was looking for.  Nevertheless, she stepped into the room, her aspect bold and filled with purpose.  It was a peculiar sight, to be sure, and yet something seemed very familiar about this woman’s gait.  Bramton fancied himself a connoisseur of female gaits, and he was sure he’d seen this one before, if only in a completely different context than the one she displayed now.  Unfortunately, he couldn’t get a clear view of the woman’s face to confirm his suspicions.
<br />

<br />
Craning his neck, Bramton watched closely as she rounded the bar’s end.  Reaching a hand below the overhang, she pulled back a wooden stool and began to sit down.
<br />

<br />
“It couldn’t be,” he muttered to himself as he watched her every move.  
<br />

<br />
The way she sat by gently lifting one knee and gliding fluidly onto the stool struck another chord with him.  Very few women he knew ever mastered the art of innocent seduction, and this one performed it with practiced ease.  By all indications, she hadn’t even realized she’d done it.  
<br />

<br />
The more he observed how she moved and behaved, the more convinced he became that he knew this person.  Bramton waited patiently for the final tell, which came when the woman unfastened the skullcap she wore, and lifted it from her head.  Tendrils of long, white hair fell to her shoulders in the same uniquely limp fashion he remembered it always did.  There was no longer any doubt in his mind—only one of two people could possibly be sitting at his bar at that moment, and judging by the person’s demeanor, he had a very good idea who it was.  Try as he might, he could hardly believe his eyes.
<br />

<br />
Pushing himself away from the doorframe, Bramton walked straight over to meet her.
<br />

<br />
“What in hate’s name are you doing here?” he spoke from behind.  The woman’s reaction was slow and measured as she sat upright at the sound of his voice.  “Someone has a lot of nerve darkening my door after the things she said when she left four months ago.”
<br />

<br />
“Four and a half,” the woman replied over her shoulder.
<br />

<br />
“What happened?” Bramton pressed.  “Things get too tough for you on the outside?  I always thought you’d come crawling back at the first sound of your stomach growling.”
<br />

<br />
“Not now, Bram.”
<br />

<br />
The man shook his head silently in awe of her raw temerity.  
<br />

<br />
“Or maybe you just miss the place, hmm?” he continued smugly.  “Somewhere under that cold skin of yours beats a sentimental heart, is that it?  Hey, I don’t blame you.  Go ahead.  Climb up and wiggle your jig for old times’ sake, if you like.  Tell you what—give me ten minutes, and I’m sure I can dig up one of your old regulars…”
<br />

<br />
That got the reaction he was fishing for.  The woman swung around on her stool to face him, her expression a mask of sheer malevolence—or would have been, had it not come from one so deceptively girlish.  The contradiction made Bramton smile that overbearing, self-satisfied smile of his.
<br />

<br />
“That’s it, isn’t it, Chi?  You got homesick.”  Bramton covered his heart with both hands mockingly.  “I have to say, I’m really quite moved that you came back to me.”
<br />

<br />
“I’m not back,” the woman insisted.  “I’m thirsty.”
<br />

<br />
The man’s hands dropped to his sides, his expression turning serious and gravely discontented as he took in her resolute attitude without a word.
<br />

<br />
“I’m here as a customer <span style="font-style: italic">only</span>, get it?” she added.
<br />

<br />
The man made a show of looking her up and down before speaking again.  “Fine.  Money up,” he shrugged, folding his arms.  
<br />

<br />
Though outwardly passive, Bramton’s tone made it clear that he was by no means pleased to see her.  In fact, had he his druthers, he would sooner have had her sorry, two-faced ass dragged up the stairs and booted out into the alley outside.  But in the end, business was business.  If she could make it worth his while, he was willing to make certain concessions—albeit reluctantly.
<br />

<br />
Reaching to her belt, the woman known as “Chi” pulled out a small pouch and tossed it onto the bar in front of her with a chink.  Reaching out an index finger, Bramton pulled down the pouch’s lip to catch a glimpse of silver shining inside.  He also thought he could see a glimmer of gold inside, but before he could pull the aperture open any further, a metal-clad hand slapped itself down on top of his, stopping him short.  Looking up into her stalwart blue eyes, the man’s perennial self-satisfied smile made its second appearance, in turn giving her the urge to hit it squarely with her chain-covered fist.
<br />

<br />
“What will it be, Miss Chianna?” he asked with a momentary bout of over-the-top cordiality.  “They still call you that, I hope?  You haven’t moved on to any more colorful pseudonyms?”
<br />

<br />
“Bourbon, straight,” she replied, pulling off her gloves.
<br />

<br />
Giving the girl one last dubious scan, Bramton skirted behind the bar to fill her order.  Selecting a bottle of midline quality, he turned back to find her sitting there, eyes closed with the bridge of her nose pinched between thumb and forefinger.  
<br />

<br />
“You know,” he began, pouring her drink into a glass, “I don’t think you appreciate just how many taverns there are in Neriak that have absolutely no idea how full of crap you are.  Of all the places to come to, you return to this… I believe the term was ‘den of scale-tongued perverts,’ wasn’t it?”
<br />

<br />
Without comment, Chianna tossed back the drink as if it were water.
<br />

<br />
“Yes…” Bramton continued.  “Yes, that was it.  I remember vividly, because that’s what you said moments before you swung back and kicked me square in the-“
<br />

<br />
“Bram, I’m sorry, okay?” the girl interrupted.  “Is that what you want to hear?”
<br />

<br />
“I was laid out for a week, Chi!”
<br />

<br />
“You’ve been out of action longer over swallowing a bad clam!”
<br />

<br />
“Oh, here we go!” Bramton threw up his arms, drawing attention from several of the patrons.  “Slip’ry Chi, Treasure Hunter extraordinaire, comes back to her roots to show us all what a huge success she is by waving silver—not platinum—on my bar and copping the exact same attitude she did when she left!  Grand… just grand.”
<br />

<br />
Chianna folded her arms peevishly, not saying a word.
<br />

<br />
“So what happened out there, huh?” the man raved.  “What happened to the bright new future you and that hussy twin of yours were supposed to make for yourselves?  Where’s the fortune?  Where’s the tales of conquest?  Where’s the big heist you two were going to pull off?  Hmm?”
<br />

<br />
“Shianna’s dead,” the girl muttered.
<br />

<br />
Bramton stopped mid-rant to blink down at Chianna.  “What?”
<br />

<br />
Pressing her lips firmly together, she looked up squarely into the man’s eyes, her own starting to fill with tears.  
<br />

<br />
“Shianna… is… dead,” she said succinctly.
<br />

<br />
“What are you talking about?” Bramton responded in disbelief.  “What happened?  When?”
<br />

<br />
The muscles in Chianna’s neck twitched as she fought to retain what tenuous grasp she had on her composure.  
<br />

<br />
“Late last week,” she whispered, looking away at the entrance to avoid his eyes.  “On our way here from the west.”
<br />

<br />
“The west…” Closing his eyes, Bramton took in a deep breath through his nose as he placed both hands against the bar top, his head lowered.  “Kithicor?” he asked, his voice muffled.
<br />

<br />
Chianna blinked involuntarily, causing a tear to stream down her cheek.
<br />

<br />
“You entered Kithicor at night,” the man accused, his head popping up to confront her.  “What were you after this time?  A gem?  A statue?  Some trinket dropped by a noble’s harlot a thousand years ago?  Of all the brainless, reckless, stupid-“
<br />

<br />
Biting his lower lip, he lowered his head again to collect himself before he lost his temper and exploded in an all-out tirade.  Chianna covered her eyes, shielding herself from the man’s pent-up rage, her shoulders heaving noiseless sobs born of guilt, loss and shame.
<br />

<br />
Raising his head, Bramton glanced around the room to see if anyone had taken undue notice of their exchange.  Good… he’d caught himself in time.  
<br />

<br />
“Come with me,” he said.
<br />

<br />
Grabbing the bottle in one hand, he scooped up the distraught girl’s arm with the other and stridently led her through the doorway he’d been leaning against earlier.
<br />

<br />
“What are you doing?” she asked, seeing that they were heading into backrooms.
<br />

<br />
“Shut up,” Bramton muttered.
<br />

<br />
“I’m not going back there-“
<br />

<br />
“Shut… up,” he repeated, pausing only to give her a cowering look.
<br />

<br />
Swallowing, Chianna closed her mouth and allowed herself to be led through the short hallway harboring three ill-fated doors.  She knew all too well the purpose behind those rooms, and was loath to revisit the skeletons they evoked, both for her and her sister.  
<br />

<br />
The thought crossed her mind of fleeing the situation altogether, believing it was a mistake—fallacy—that she even considered coming back here.  But for all her mental posturing, she merely stood there hugging herself uncomfortably as Bramton let go her arm to unlock the door farthest from the common room.  
<br />

<br />
A jingle of the keys later, the door swung open on creaky hinges.  The remembered smell of mildew emerged from the room like a specter, further adding to Chianna’s headlong rush into a personal history she would sorely have preferred to leave in the past.  
<br />

<br />
Again she considered rushing out of there, but before she could clearly decide what to do, Bramton again took her by the arm and led the way inside where he tossed her carelessly to the bed.  Kicking the door shut behind him, he stepped to one side, his back turned to her.
<br />

<br />
“What do you think you’re doing!” Chianna demanded as she scooted angrily to the edge of the mattress.  “If you think for one second I’m…”
<br />

<br />
The clink of glass reached the girl’s ears.  Brushing wayward strands of hair from her face, she glared up at the back of Bramton’s head to find he was fiddling with something beyond her view.  
<br />

<br />
“Here,” he said calmly, turning to present her with another glass of bourbon, half-filled this time from the bottle he’d brought with him.  
<br />

<br />
It was Bramton’s tone, more than anything, that caught her notice.  Looking uncertainly from the glass to the man’s face and back again, Chianna grudgingly accepted the drink.  His task done, the man replaced the cork and set the bottle atop a barrel sitting nearby.  Much to her relief, he made no move to approach her on the bed.  Instead, he merely sat back to lean against the barrel, folding his arms and crossing his ankles casually.
<br />

<br />
“Tell me what happened,” he said evenly.
<br />

<br />
Chianna looked down into the blood red liquid in the glass he gave her.  She wasn’t sure what she had expected to happen once they were alone in the musty closet-like room, but calm, deliberate conversation wasn’t it.  Instead of tossing back the liquid in a single gulp as she had done before, she favored taking a brief sip before launching into her explanation.
<br />

<br />
“It was late in the afternoon by the time we got to HighKeep,” she said, fondling the glass in her lap distractedly.  “We were in good spirits, having just swindled the Karana bandits for all they were worth.  They didn’t suspect a thing, and we were feeling pretty happy with ourselves.”
<br />

<br />
“What was the scam?” Bramton asked.
<br />

<br />
Chianna licked her lips before answering, still not looking up at him.  “You remember that dice trick you showed us?”
<br />

<br />
The man shifted his ankles so they crossed the other way.  “I remember.”
<br />

<br />
“Shianna…” the girl reminisced, forcing a smile in spite of herself.  “You should have seen her.  She was a pro, keeping those petty humans distracted while I made each switch.  She even turned <span style="font-style: italic">my </span>head once or twice.”  With a half-chuckle, she looked up to find the man’s serious expression aimed down at her.
<br />

<br />
“It was a good day’s take,” she resumed, looking again down at the glass.  “We decided to celebrate that night by parlaying our good fortune in the Keep’s casino.”
<br />

<br />
Clenching his jaw, Bramton saw where this was going, but let the girl continue without interrupting.
<br />

<br />
“Things went well the first few hours, but we got cocky,” she admitted, taking another sip.  “One of the humans at the table caught the last switch.  The next thing we knew, a tall barbarian brute had us head-locked under each arm, marching us clear to the edge of Highpass Hold… on the east side... where he just dumped us and walked away.”
<br />

<br />
Chianna paused, taking a longer pull at her drink, and sighing a tremulous breath as she wiped at the corner of her mouth with the back of her hand.
<br />

<br />
“We didn’t have a chance out there at that hour,” she muttered.  “That barbarian bastard knew it, too.”
<br />

<br />
“You’re Teir’Dal,” Bramton shrugged.  “You were lucky they didn’t just kill you both on the spot.”
<br />

<br />
“You think that matters to Shianna right now?” she asked, looking up solemnly.  “I can’t help thinking that… if only we hadn’t stayed up so late.  If only my hand was quicker.  If only-” Her voice seized as she was overcome by bitter self-recrimination.
<br />

<br />
“You’ll ‘if only’ yourself until the day you die, Chi.  I know it’s not easy to hear, but Shianna made the same fool choices you did.  She knew the risks.  You weren’t her keeper.”
<br />

<br />
“Then why am I alive when she is <span style="font-style: italic">dead</span>!” she spat back at him, her lower lip taut and quivering.  Another tear escaped her control and fell quickly down her cheek as she sat there glaring at the man across from her.  “Don’t talk to me about luck when half of me lies dead back in that forest!”
<br />

<br />
“It’s better than all of you,” Bramton argued, shoving a finger her way.  “And whether you like it or not, you <span style="font-style: italic">are </span>alive.  I don’t know, maybe you deserve to be lying dead in Kithicor right now, but the fact is you’re right here.  You live.  You breathe.  Nothing is going to change that unless you decide to.  Is that what you want?”
<br />

<br />
With a sullen swipe of her hand, Chianna wiped the tear trail from her cheek.  “No.”
<br />

<br />
“Then take rapture in your life.  You got a second chance she didn’t.  What would she tell you to do with it if she were here in this room now?”
<br />

<br />
Bramton didn’t expect an answer, nor did the girl offer one.  She merely closed her eyes, her brow furrowed at the dozens of things going through her head at that moment.  
<br />

<br />
“Do you have a place to stay?” he asked quietly.
<br />

<br />
“Except for what I’m wearing,” she shrugged, gesturing down at herself, “some lucky human in HighKeep has the pleasure of all my assets.”
<br />
 
<br />
“That’s not entirely true, is it?”  Bramton wore his self-satisfied smile again.  “Your <span style="font-style: italic">natural </span>assets await you here, if you’re smart enough to let them serve you.”  Upon seeing the stark anger flash across her face, he quickly amended, “Just long enough to get you back on your feet.”  
<br />

<br />
“Get this through your head.” Chianna stood slowly to confront the man.  “I am never doing that again—not for you or anyone.  I would sooner fall on my own blade than submit myself to some drunken wretch in this hellhole.”
<br />

<br />
“It’s the only way you’re staying here,” he said bluntly.  “I can’t have one of my rooms occupied all night unless it’s making a tidy profit.”
<br />

<br />
Chianna stared at him blankly.  “I don’t know why I expected anything else,” she stated flatly.
<br />

<br />
“Me either,” he shrugged, returning her stare with deliberate equanimity.
<br />

<br />
“Fine.”  She shoved the nearly empty glass into his hands.  “If you need anything, I’ll be out back.”
<br />

<br />
“Oh come now, Slip’ry Chi,” Bramton cajoled wryly as she yanked the door open.  “You can’t seriously tell me you prefer the alley to a warm bed.”
<br />

<br />
“Wouldn’t be the first time,” she replied.
<br />

<br />
“Alone?”
<br />

<br />
That stopped her.  Bramton knew she was no stranger to spending the night in alleyways or the odd nook when the need arose—it was how she found herself in his employ to begin with.  But until now, such adversity had always been in the omnipresent company of her twin sister.  This night, only the company of rats and damp stone awaited her in the cold, uncaring Dark Elf city.
<br />

<br />
Rather than respond, Chianna merely glowered at the man still leaning back against the barrel.  Raising the glass in a mock salute, he downed the rest of the bourbon she’d left behind.
<br />

<br />
“Sooner or later, you <span style="font-style: italic">will </span>come back to me,” he said with complete confidence.
<br />

<br />
Jaw clenched, Chianna silently took her leave of the loathsome room and its vile occupant, closing the door solidly behind her.<br />_________________<br />- Slip'ry Chi'
